A clickable card's `@click` reaches its handler on every target, and a card
that is not clickable takes no press.

Every override took the card's content and nothing else, so a `@click` written
on one was dropped without a word. android renders Material's clickable Card,
the overload taking `onClick`. html marks the box a button, with `role`,
`tabindex` and a pointer, and listens for the click. fyne shows the box in a
fynelayout.Tap, gtk4 the frame in a frameless GtkButton.

The other targets' overrides branch on `clickable` with the content under
each branch. A call site writing no `clickable` leaves that `if` on a literal
once the override is substituted, and passReactivity gave the branch not
taken a render slot of its own for the `if` the plain card holds; codegen
dropped the branch and kept the calls re-rendering into it, a node never
built -- on gtk4 a field the Model does not declare. passInlinePure keeps only
the branch taken.
bubbletea focuses controls rather than regions, so a clickable card is its
content beside a `›` that takes the focus and fires on enter; a test fires it
through the card's id, which is on the card's box rather than on the `›`.

The cards under the loop are reached a copy at a time, `c.row[1]`.

deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `Card\(onClick = [^)]*\{\s*\}` -- the plain card is not made clickable
-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import "sngl:test"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
    none {
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

component main ui.node {
    var opened = ""

    ui.vbox {
        for var name = ["one", "two"] {
            ui.card #row(clickable=true, @click { opened = name }) {
                ui.text(value=name)
            }
        }
        ui.card(variant="outlined") {
            if opened != "" {
                ui.text(value="opened {opened}")
            }
        }
    }
}

func testClickOpens(t test.Test, c main) {
    c.row[1].click()
    t.assert(c.opened == "two")
    c.row[0].click()
    t.assert(c.opened == "one")
}

ui.window {
    main
}
